{"name":"Wood Charcoal","hex":"#464646","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"},{"term":"charcoal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["impure carbon obtained by destructive distillation of wood or other organic matter, that is, heating it in the absence of oxygen.","A stick of black carbon material used for drawing."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark gray colour.","Made of charcoal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/charcoal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood"},{"title":"Charcoal","blocks":[{"text":"Charcoal is a lightweight black residue made of carbon that is produced by strongly heating wood in minimal oxygen to remove all water and volatile constituents. In the traditional version of this pyrolysis process, called charcoal burning, often by forming a charcoal kiln, the heat is supplied by burning part of the starting material itself, with a limited supply of oxygen. The material can also be heated in a closed retort. Modern charcoal briquettes used for outdoor cooking may contain many other additives, e.g. coal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Charcoal_8.jpg/330px-Charcoal_8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charcoal"},{"title":"Shades of gray","blocks":[{"text":"Variations of gray or grey include achromatic grayscale shades, which lie exactly between white and black, and nearby colors with low colorfulness. A selection of a number of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Shades_of_Gray.svg/330px-Shades_of_Gray.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_gray"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/500px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/1280px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/500px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A knot on a tree trunk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATreeKnot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wood_Knot.JPG/500px-Wood_Knot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wood_Knot.JPG/1280px-Wood_Knot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wood_Knot.JPG/500px-Wood_Knot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_